From 7507fb76510421b9f1207b431b3cbd3388241f49 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ville=20Kivela=CC=88?= <vkivela@hotmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:11:04 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] interceptor: let a request interceptor ask for the host's DNS
 aliases

A content blocker judges a request by its host, and a tracker served
from a subdomain of the page's own site, with a DNS CNAME pointing at
the tracker's real host, never shows the tracker's name. The
interceptor cannot resolve the host itself: it runs before the network
stack, and a lookup of its own would use a different resolver, cache
and secure DNS configuration from the request's.

QWebEngineUrlRequestInfo::requestDnsAliases() asks for them. When an
interceptor calls it and neither blocks nor redirects the request, the
host is resolved through the profile's own network context, and the
interceptor is called once more with dnsAliases() set to the names in
the host's CNAME chain. The resolver keeps those in a sorted set, so
the list says which names are in the chain and not which is canonical.
The lookup goes through the profile's host cache, so the request that
follows reads the answer rather than resolving again.

A profile also keeps its answers for a minute, so a page's later
requests to the same host wait for neither the proxy check nor the
lookup. Answers are kept per profile, because they say which hosts the
profile has visited, and only after a proxy check that allowed a
lookup, so a proxy set since cannot be bypassed.

No lookup is made for a main-frame navigation, for an IP address, or
when a proxy applies to the request: a local lookup behind a proxy
leaks the query and can disagree with the address the proxy connects
to. A failed lookup, or one that names only the host itself, makes no
second call, and the first call's decision stands. An interceptor that
never asks sees no change.

Tests stand in for the resolver through a private hook that answers
after the proxy check, so a CNAME chain can be named without a DNS
server and the proxy case still proves no lookup is made.
